verification.strongest_defensible_claim:
**What holds.** In both FT runs (ours n=31; DSSE's n=92), the FT-side feature that tracks 14B transfer most strongly and most robustly is answer length:
- at most 40 words: rho about +0.6;
- it holds in the 60 datasets we did not select (+0.41 [+0.16, +0.62]);
- it is not cherry-picked: rho stays between +0.53 and +0.62 for cut-offs from 20 to 80 words;
- it survives controlling for whether each answer is wrong (+0.57 in DSSE n=92).

Wrongness carries smaller extra signal on top (DSSE n=92: +0.37 to +0.39 after controlling for length). In our 31 datasets wrongness and length cannot be separated at the dataset level (rho -0.94).

**answer_first is mostly a length proxy, not "essentially" one.** About 91% of answer_first-true answers have at most 40 words. Once length is controlled non-parametrically, a modest residual remains: +0.40 (ours), +0.36 (DSSE n=92), +0.22 (DSSE n=60).

**"Real early commitment does not track 14B" is not supported.** The raw null comes from the measure itself. Early commitment needs at least 20 words after the answer, so the short answers typical of high-transfer datasets cannot count. The length mix alone predicts it at -0.55 (ours) and -0.51 (DSSE n=92). After removing that length dependence, early commitment tracks 14B at a modest +0.3 to +0.5, with CIs excluding 0 in all three samples. It is a stable trait (cross-run +0.73), but rare: about 5% of FT answers.

**Corrected statement.** High-transfer datasets make FT answers short. At any given length they are also somewhat more likely to state the answer first and justify it afterwards. Length is the dominant axis, but it is not the only one.

One of 96 k16 contexts (08152 seed 0) produced a large, real context-driven switch. It is not sampling luck: 73/100 answers are terse where no other run exceeds 4/100, and an independent in-domain generation from the same context is also extreme (52 words vs the next-lowest 147). The switch reproduces the math-accuracy part of the FT side effect, as averaged across datasets:
- wrong 0.66 vs FT 0.65;
- terse answers wrong at the same rate as FT's terse answers, above what problem difficulty predicts;
- FT-like error anatomy (few near misses);
- an FT-like, not ICL-like, pattern of which problems fail.

It is not the complete FT phenotype:
- it keeps the base model's markdown (72% bold vs 0.1% under FT);
- it is more answer-first and more terse than typical FT;
- it does not resemble its own dataset's FT run, which is among the least affected (wrong 0.41, 64 words).

Across the other 95 contexts, the effect of shot draws on wrong is not detectable (overdispersion 1.11, p=0.20). The effect over shot draws is therefore best described as a rare discrete switch (about 1/96, CI 0.0003-0.057), not a continuous heavy tail. It occurred in the only one of all 92 datasets whose rows are rich in explicit brevity requests (30/150), so it may be specific to that dataset.

The jackpot does dominate variance-based statistics: it drives the ICC of ICL wrong from 0.31 to 0.00 and is the most influential single run on icl~ft wrong and icl~ft le2. But it works against the ICL-FT correspondence. Rank-based conclusions ("ICL wrong does not track FT wrong or 14B transfer") hold without it and by median: wrong~ft 0.09 [-0.27, 0.42], wrong~14B 0.15 [-0.27, 0.54]. Without it, the 3-seed means are still only moderately reliable for wrong (about 0.58) and poorly reliable for le2 and answer_first (ICC <= 0.18). So it is not "what makes the dataset means unreliable"; it is what drives the variance-based ICC to zero.
